1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,648
Narrator: Scenario 34. Cloud platforms, containers, and automation.

2
00:00:07,248 --> 00:00:17,088
B: The replacement pods have been pending for fifteen minutes. We have free memory elsewhere in the cluster, so why can the scheduler not place them?

3
00:00:17,688 --> 00:00:29,232
A: The scheduler reports insufficient requested memory on eligible nodes. These pods require the production application pool, and the spare memory is in a different pool.

4
00:00:29,832 --> 00:00:39,120
B: Could we lower the memory requests to make them fit? The dashboard shows only moderate usage, and the release window ends in an hour.

5
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:50,856
A: We need peak demand and startup measurements, not just the current average. Lower requests could cause overpacking and leave the workload vulnerable during load spikes.

6
00:00:51,456 --> 00:01:02,136
B: The new version uses the same requests as the previous one. Is the rolling update temporarily asking for more capacity because old replicas remain active?

7
00:01:02,736 --> 00:01:14,760
A: Yes, the configured surge adds two pods while keeping existing replicas available. I also checked placement rules; the application must remain distributed across two zones.

8
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:26,472
B: What is the lowest-risk path within this window? We could change the update strategy, add eligible nodes, or postpone the release until capacity is ready.

9
00:01:27,072 --> 00:01:38,472
A: The node pool has approved quota for one additional node per zone. Provisioning them preserves the current availability settings, provided the nodes become ready in time.

10
00:01:39,072 --> 00:01:48,720
B: Please start the approved capacity change. At what point should we stop waiting and defer the release rather than compress the validation period?

11
00:01:49,320 --> 00:02:01,416
A: If both nodes are not ready by 15:30 UTC, we will defer. Ready means cluster registration, network checks, and sufficient allocatable memory all pass.

12
00:02:02,016 --> 00:02:13,704
B: I will communicate that checkpoint to the release team. Once scheduling succeeds, we still need application readiness and the planned business validation before proceeding.

13
00:02:14,304 --> 00:02:25,104
A: I will own node validation and capture the scheduler events. After this release, we will include rollout surge capacity in our routine capacity planning.
